OpenSSL 4.1.0: on an ECH rejection the client sends ech_required before its own flight

Kuicksilver's own code, the eighth patch carried under ADR-0019 (docs/adr/0019-openssl-4-and-ech.md), applied after
openssl-4.1.0-ech-grease-length.patch; pinned by its SHA-256 in gradle/openssl.toml as patch_8, applied by
tools/openssl/apply-patch.pl to the verified tarball, and dropped with the first OpenSSL release that fixes it (upstream
issue https://github.com/openssl/openssl/issues/33831, which carries this fix and a reproducer). Applies to OpenSSL
master at 3a3f13563825 too (patch(1) with offsets).

The bug: RFC 9849 section 6.1.6 has a client whose ECH was rejected complete the handshake, authenticated for the
public_name, and then abort with ech_required "before sending any application data"; section 6.1.7: "If the server
requests a client certificate, the client MUST respond with an empty Certificate message, denoting no client
certificate." tls_process_initial_server_flight (statem_clnt.c) sends ech_required while the server's Finished is
processed, before the client's flight: no Certificate, no Finished, and with SSL_OP_ENABLE_MIDDLEBOX_COMPAT the alert
leaves in a plaintext record.

The fix: tls_prepare_client_certificate takes the "no certificate" path (cert_req 2; neither cert_cb nor
client_cert_cb is called) when ECH was offered and rejected, so the Certificate is empty and no CertificateVerify is
sent; tls_process_initial_server_flight only marks the rejection (reject_pending); the client post-work after its
Finished, under the application write keys, sends ech_required (retry_configs_ok set as before).

diff --git a/ssl/ech/ech_local.h b/ssl/ech/ech_local.h
